# 📖 Overview

ResponseGuard AI is an NLP application designed to evaluate the quality of AI-generated responses.

Given:

- A user question
- Supporting context
- An AI-generated response

the model predicts whether the response is:

- ✅ Factual
- ❌ Contradictory
- ⚪ Irrelevant

The objective of this project is not only to build an accurate classifier, but also to demonstrate the complete Machine Learning Engineering lifecycle by evolving the model into a deployable AI application.

---

# 🚀 Current Features

### Data Processing

- Automatic JSON dataset loading
- Missing value handling
- Label preprocessing
- Text cleaning

### Feature Engineering

- Combined Question + Context + Answer representation
- TF-IDF Vectorization
- Unigram & Bigram Features
- Chi-Square Feature Selection

### Model Development

- Logistic Regression
- Balanced Class Weights
- Stratified Cross Validation
- Hyperparameter Optimization
- Macro F1 Optimization

### Prediction

- Batch inference
- JSON prediction export
- Label decoding

---

# 💡 Why ResponseGuard AI?

Large Language Models can occasionally generate responses that are:

- Factually incorrect
- Contradictory
- Irrelevant

ResponseGuard AI explores how traditional NLP techniques can automatically evaluate response quality before responses reach end users.

The long-term goal is to build a production-ready AI quality assessment system that can integrate into chatbot workflows through a REST API and modern web interface.

---

# 📚 Dataset

The project uses a response classification dataset containing:

- User Question
- Supporting Context
- AI-generated Response
- Ground Truth Label

Target Classes

- Factual
- Contradictory
- Irrelevant

Each training example combines the question, context, and response into a single textual representation before feature extraction.

---

# 🧠 Model

Current implementation:

- TF-IDF Vectorizer
- Chi-Square Feature Selection
- Logistic Regression
- GridSearchCV
- Stratified K-Fold Cross Validation

This baseline was selected because it provides fast training, strong interpretability, and efficient inference while establishing a solid benchmark for future improvements.

Future versions may evaluate transformer-based models for comparison.

---

# 📈 Current Development

The initial machine learning pipeline has been successfully implemented.

Current work focuses on:

- Improving evaluation
